WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.675
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.675 --> 00:00:04.050
JEFF HOFFMAN: It's an exciting
time, the last few minutes

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:08.230
before launch, as the
shuttle really comes alive,

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:11.130
and then all of a sudden you
get this big kick in the pants,

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:13.110
and you look out the
window, and there's

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:16.440
the ground falling away,
and everything is shaking.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.090
SARAH HANSEN: Today
on Chalk Radio,

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:20.627
we're heading back into space.

00:00:20.627 --> 00:00:22.710
JEFF HOFFMAN: My best
friend from the second grade

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:24.780
after I became an astronaut--

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.720
he said to me, Jeff,
this is incredible.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.670
I used to play
spaceman with you,

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:33.390
and now you're actually
going up into space.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:35.520
SARAH HANSEN: I'm your
host, Sarah Hansen,

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.130
and with us this week is
Professor Jeff Hoffman,

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:40.260
a former NASA astronaut.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.240
Over the course
of five missions,

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.300
he's logged over
1,000 hours in space--

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:48.900
and yep, some of those
do include spacewalks.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.810
Professor Hoffman teaches
Aeronautics and Astronautics

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:54.570
at MIT, but he's also
committed to unlocking

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:56.400
this knowledge for the world.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.700
He's shared course 16.00,
Introduction to Aerospace

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:03.300
Engineering, on
MITx, and 16.885,

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.480
Engineering the Space Shuttle,
which can be found on MITx

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.340
and OCW.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:11.190
One of the things I loved about
my conversation with Professor

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:14.100
Hoffman was his
take on perspective,

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.410
and how a grander-scale view--

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:18.300
like, say, from outer space--

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:22.900
can dramatically shift the
way we live here on Earth.

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:24.650
JEFF HOFFMAN: We fly
so much in airplanes,

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:26.420
and probably most
people don't remember

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:29.600
the first time they look
out of an airplane window

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:30.650
and see the Earth.

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:33.330
I mean, it's a completely
different perspective

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:34.010
that you get.

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:36.620
And now rockets
and space travel,

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:38.870
which allow us to
go even farther

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:43.460
and to try to give people
the perspective of looking

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:45.410
at the Earth from space--

00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:47.480
is very important.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:49.820
I mean, I was an astronomer.

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:51.470
I know the Earth is a planet.

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:53.270
I mean, give me a break.

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:57.110
But nevertheless, when
you actually see it

00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:59.540
from the perspective
of being in space--

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:02.240
I mean, imagine you
go up into space

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:06.380
and you look at this thin,
blue line on the horizon, which

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:08.210
is the Earth's atmosphere.

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:10.400
I mean, you go out
here from the surface

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:14.150
of the Earth on a clear
day in the big blue sky,

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:16.400
and it looks like
it goes on forever.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.510
And unfortunately, that
gives many people the idea

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:23.060
that it doesn't matter what
you put into the atmosphere,

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.533
because it's so big
that it can absorb it.

00:02:25.533 --> 00:02:27.200
And it's the same
thing with the oceans.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:32.570
And when you finally
get that gut feeling

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:35.650
of the finiteness of the Earth--

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:38.660
the oceans, the atmosphere--

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:41.690
and you realize we live
in a closed system,

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:45.500
that the Earth is
a finite system--

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:50.390
that makes a difference, because
exponential growth cannot

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:54.410
continue forever
in a closed system.

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:56.570
SARAH HANSEN: Professor
Hoffman's first expedition

00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:59.780
into space was in 1985.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:03.140
Before that, he was actually
doing work here at MIT,

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:05.570
launching telescopes into space.

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:08.690
Long before that,
though, like many kids

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:10.950
since the second half
of the 20th century,

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:13.717
he wanted to go
to space himself.

00:03:13.717 --> 00:03:15.300
JEFF HOFFMAN: When
I was a little kid,

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:19.830
I used to love looking
up at the stars.

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:22.970
Of course, early part of my
life we lived in New York City,

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:26.270
and more often, it
would be looking up

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:28.040
at the stars in the planetarium.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:30.260
But I was fascinated with that.

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:34.010
And it was the dawn
of the Space Age,

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:39.480
and there were lots of articles
in newspapers, magazines,

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:43.370
television shows about the
coming of the Space Age.

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:46.520
And I ended up--

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:49.850
in fact, my best friend
from the second grade,

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:51.950
after I became an astronaut--

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:55.700
he said to me, you know,
Jeff, this is incredible.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:57.650
I used to play
spaceman with you,

00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:00.080
and now you're actually
going up into space.

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:04.370
It was a combination of the
fascination with how far

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:08.720
away things were, and the
fact that by going up,

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:11.570
you got a completely
new view of things.

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:15.320
And also that space travel
represented the future,

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:17.670
and I found that
really exciting.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:22.370
So then when the first
astronauts started to fly,

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:25.940
they were our heroes,
and I just wanted

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:27.740
to go up there and look down.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:35.630
Unfortunately, I soon realized
that all the astronauts

00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:39.650
were military pilots, and that
was not really a career for me.

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:45.260
But that desire to look further
out, to understand what's

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:47.630
above, what's out there--

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:52.250
that remained with me, and
so I ended up with a degree--

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:54.650
advanced degrees
in astrophysics.

00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:59.210
And I was launching
telescopes to look at X-rays--

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:01.920
and you have to go above the
atmosphere to look at X-rays.

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:04.040
That's what I was
doing here at MIT.

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:08.570
Then NASA started to
develop what was then

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:10.070
the brand new Space Shuttle.

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:15.440
Now we're up to the late '70s,
and the thing about the Shuttle

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:17.990
was they had a crew of
seven, and they only

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:19.340
needed two pilots.

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:21.410
So that was great,
because that's

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:23.550
what really opened
things up for scientists,

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:27.350
engineers, medical doctors, and
that's what gave me my chance.

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:30.390
And I applied for it, and was
lucky enough to get selected.

00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:33.950
And so then I could get the
real overview from space.

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:38.700
SARAH HANSEN: Five
missions later,

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:41.280
and Professor Hoffman
is back on Earth.

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:43.710
And since 2001,
he's been teaching

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:45.450
a series of courses
on aeronautics

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:47.790
and engineering here at MIT.

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:49.620
Luckily for us,
Professor Hoffman

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:52.650
infuses much of his NASA
experiences and learnings

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:54.570
into his courses.

00:05:54.570 --> 00:05:57.750
To help the students really
understand the big picture--

00:05:57.750 --> 00:05:59.520
like he had to at NASA--

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.930
he emphasizes the concept
of systems engineering.

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:06.000
Understanding
systems engineering

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.040
helps students and
astronauts alike

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:10.710
to tackle complex
problems, and it all

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:13.662
starts by looking at
the bigger picture.

00:06:13.662 --> 00:06:15.120
JEFF HOFFMAN:
Particularly anything

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:20.760
having to do with space, with
complex systems in general.

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:23.670
We try to teach systems
engineering here.

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:26.190
You have to have the
big picture if you're

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:28.890
going to be able to manage
a complex engineering

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:31.500
or scientific project.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:34.470
In the AeroAstro
Department at MIT,

00:06:34.470 --> 00:06:38.250
we don't really teach
systems engineering

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:43.590
as a specific subject until the
graduate level, because really,

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:46.740
if you're going to be a
professional systems engineer

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:50.400
and be good at it,
you really need

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:53.850
a background in some of
the basics of engineering.

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:57.180
So just like at
NASA Mission Control

00:06:57.180 --> 00:06:59.790
you don't start out
being a flight director.

00:06:59.790 --> 00:07:03.750
You start out working in one
of the individual disciplines.

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:07.440
And usually, the most
successful flight directors

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:09.300
have worked in at
least two disciplines.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:13.620
And then you understand how a
disciplined engineer approaches

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:17.100
a problem, and then you
can be more useful trying

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:20.918
to get the big view of things.

00:07:20.918 --> 00:07:22.710
SARAH HANSEN: Another
really wonderful part

00:07:22.710 --> 00:07:26.160
of Professor Hoffman's courses
is how they combine theory

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:29.397
with real hands-on experience.

00:07:29.397 --> 00:07:31.230
JEFF HOFFMAN: The
majority of our students--

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:33.300
they are going to be
working engineers.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:35.470
They're going to go
out into the world.

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:38.310
Many of them get jobs
in aerospace companies,

00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:40.920
and they're going to build
the next generation of space

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:42.060
vehicles.

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:46.950
And we want them to have
had some experience actually

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:48.270
building something.

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:53.400
For instance, here, I teach the
freshman course in introduction

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:56.520
to aerospace engineering
and human spaceflight,

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:59.640
and we have the students
build a project--

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:00.840
a lighter-than-air vehicle.

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:03.990
We give them the material.

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:07.050
We give them some radio
control equipment,

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:13.770
motors, servos, propellers,
and lead them through,

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:19.290
in a systems engineering way
of going about the project,

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:22.050
without formally telling
them that they're

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:24.730
practicing systems engineering.

00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:27.990
I mean, even if you're going
to be a disciplined engineer

00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:31.290
and just concentrate on
one aspect of a system,

00:08:31.290 --> 00:08:36.360
it still helps to have
the concept that somehow

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:38.190
all these systems
have to play together.

00:08:40.924 --> 00:08:43.049
SARAH HANSEN: Another way
Professor Hoffman ensures

00:08:43.049 --> 00:08:49.140
that these ideas really sink in
is with something called CDIO.

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:53.727
CDIO stands for Conceive,
Design, Implement, and Operate,

00:08:53.727 --> 00:08:55.560
and it's a learning
tool that helps students

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:59.490
to think through a problem "from
soup to nuts," as he puts it.

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:01.530
What's wonderful about
it-- beyond the fact that

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:04.470
it's helped us put humans
into space and on the moon--

00:09:04.470 --> 00:09:06.910
is that it can be useful in
tons of different contexts,

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:08.642
even beyond engineering.

00:09:08.642 --> 00:09:11.100
JEFF HOFFMAN: The bottom line,
and the moral of all of this

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:14.460
is you really need,
from the very beginning,

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:18.480
when you're designing a system,
to think about the end user.

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:21.180
Very often, the people
who design it never

00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:22.530
have to use the system.

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:25.350
They come up with a design, and
they throw it over the fence,

00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:27.090
and somebody else builds it.

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:29.590
And they don't have to use it,
they throw it over the fence,

00:09:29.590 --> 00:09:31.720
and then the end
user takes over.

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:33.310
And so it's really critical.

00:09:33.310 --> 00:09:37.290
So the CDIO-- it's
a way of approaching

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:41.280
engineering particularly
complex systems

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:42.990
that, from the
very beginning, you

00:09:42.990 --> 00:09:47.310
take into account all of
those phases of an engineering

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:48.240
system.

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:51.330
I mean, it's not just
engineering systems.

00:09:51.330 --> 00:09:56.130
When you build a house, I
mean, you better figure out

00:09:56.130 --> 00:09:58.320
what it's going to be
to live in this house

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:00.120
before it gets built.
Otherwise you're

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:03.570
going to have a lot of bills
with a contractor to make

00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:04.980
changes at the last minute.

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:06.397
And that's one of
the other things

00:10:06.397 --> 00:10:09.240
you learn, and one of the
basic principles of systems

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:11.550
engineering-- and particularly
in the space business--

00:10:11.550 --> 00:10:15.690
is that the further you
get into the implementation

00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:17.940
of the system,
the more expensive

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:19.680
it is to make any changes.

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:21.180
SARAH HANSEN: I
feel like that's one

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:22.773
of the beauties of this course.

00:10:22.773 --> 00:10:24.690
You might be working in
a completely different

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:27.840
discipline, but you can
find something useful.

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:29.910
JEFF HOFFMAN: That is
kind of the subtext

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:33.330
of the course-- is it's not
just about the Space Shuttle.

00:10:33.330 --> 00:10:39.310
It's learning how a very, very
complex system was initially

00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:43.640
conceived, designed,
implemented, and operated.

00:10:43.640 --> 00:10:47.590
And that's why, after
the central part

00:10:47.590 --> 00:10:49.840
of the course, where we
talk about the design

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:54.040
and building of all these
complex subsystems, then

00:10:54.040 --> 00:10:56.890
we brought in people who
actually operated the shuttle--

00:10:56.890 --> 00:11:01.030
the launch director, the flight
director, the payload operator,

00:11:01.030 --> 00:11:03.370
an astronaut pilot
who flew the shuttle,

00:11:03.370 --> 00:11:09.460
and so on-- because that's the
final phase of the whole CDIO.

00:11:09.460 --> 00:11:11.260
As I say, from
soup to nuts, this

00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:14.707
is what the shuttle
was all about.

00:11:14.707 --> 00:11:16.790
SARAH HANSEN: To really
understand the development

00:11:16.790 --> 00:11:19.340
of the space program
here in the US,

00:11:19.340 --> 00:11:21.890
it helps to look at the
interconnected series

00:11:21.890 --> 00:11:24.050
of systems that built it.

00:11:24.050 --> 00:11:27.530
And they go well beyond
engineering and mathematics.

00:11:27.530 --> 00:11:30.650
For example, politics
played a huge role

00:11:30.650 --> 00:11:33.020
in aerospace engineering.

00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:34.670
JEFF HOFFMAN: We
really thought it

00:11:34.670 --> 00:11:37.220
was important to
devote the beginning

00:11:37.220 --> 00:11:44.990
lectures of the course to what
were all these influences which

00:11:44.990 --> 00:11:48.380
had such an important
impact on the design?

00:11:48.380 --> 00:11:52.570
Economically, it was no
longer possible to treat

00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:57.690
NASA in such a special way
as had been done by Apollo.

00:11:57.690 --> 00:12:02.630
I mean, 400,000 people working
on Apollo at the height of it,

00:12:02.630 --> 00:12:06.840
4% of the federal budget
going to space exploration--

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:09.500
this is not normal
for space activities.

00:12:09.500 --> 00:12:12.370
It was a very special
time in our history.

00:12:12.370 --> 00:12:14.030
Then you get to the late '60s.

00:12:14.030 --> 00:12:17.990
I mean, even John Kennedy, after
having made this commitment

00:12:17.990 --> 00:12:20.960
to go to the moon, started to
have second thoughts about how

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:22.610
much it was going to cost.

00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:24.420
Then, of course, he
was assassinated,

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:27.350
and getting to the moon
by the end of the decade

00:12:27.350 --> 00:12:32.090
sort of became our way of
honoring Kennedy's legacy.

00:12:32.090 --> 00:12:33.140
So it wasn't a question.

00:12:33.140 --> 00:12:34.880
We did it.

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:38.120
But along came the
Nixon administration

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:42.020
following President Johnson's
Great Society program--

00:12:42.020 --> 00:12:44.780
huge increase in
social spending--

00:12:44.780 --> 00:12:47.300
and then the Vietnam War.

00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:53.240
And space-- it was clear, and
Nixon was explicit about this--

00:12:53.240 --> 00:12:57.380
the space program has
to take its proper role

00:12:57.380 --> 00:13:00.410
among all other
national priorities,

00:13:00.410 --> 00:13:04.490
and that really involved a
huge decrease in NASA's budget.

00:13:04.490 --> 00:13:07.230
And the people running
NASA saw that coming.

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:12.110
And so how do you
continue to explore space?

00:13:12.110 --> 00:13:13.910
They were faced with
the real possibility

00:13:13.910 --> 00:13:18.080
that human space flight in this
country might come to an end.

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:22.280
And NASA realized--
quite correctly--

00:13:22.280 --> 00:13:26.150
that reusability was a key.

00:13:26.150 --> 00:13:31.670
But then you get into all of
the social, economic, political

00:13:31.670 --> 00:13:37.190
influences that put a lot of
very, very tight requirements

00:13:37.190 --> 00:13:38.330
on the shuttle--

00:13:38.330 --> 00:13:44.780
particularly since, in order
to justify the expense,

00:13:44.780 --> 00:13:48.680
NASA basically
sold the shuttle as

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:51.470
capable of doing anything
we needed to do in space,

00:13:51.470 --> 00:13:52.730
we could do with a shuttle.

00:13:52.730 --> 00:13:54.770
We used it for
scientific experiments.

00:13:54.770 --> 00:13:56.360
We used it to launch satellites.

00:13:56.360 --> 00:13:59.570
We used it to test out
new engineering systems.

00:13:59.570 --> 00:14:03.470
We used it to repair satellites
like the Hubble Space

00:14:03.470 --> 00:14:03.980
Telescope.

00:14:03.980 --> 00:14:07.160
We used it to build the
International Space Station.

00:14:07.160 --> 00:14:11.000
For me, all my flights were in
the space shuttle, so I look

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:13.580
on it with a great fondness--

00:14:13.580 --> 00:14:16.700
recognizing its
limitations, but also

00:14:16.700 --> 00:14:18.770
understanding the
incredible things

00:14:18.770 --> 00:14:22.512
that it allowed
us to accomplish.

00:14:22.512 --> 00:14:24.470
SARAH HANSEN: While
Professor Hoffman's classes

00:14:24.470 --> 00:14:27.530
offer incredible insights into
what he's learned at NASA--

00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:29.630
not to mention some
deeply important skills

00:14:29.630 --> 00:14:32.540
for any student or
intrigued OCW user--

00:14:32.540 --> 00:14:36.680
it's not every day that I get to
sit down with a real astronaut.

00:14:36.680 --> 00:14:39.710
So of course I had to ask
him what it was all like--

00:14:39.710 --> 00:14:44.408
being launched into space, being
in orbit, doing a spacewalk.

00:14:44.408 --> 00:14:45.950
JEFF HOFFMAN: Before
my first launch,

00:14:45.950 --> 00:14:49.640
I asked many of the astronauts
who had already flown,

00:14:49.640 --> 00:14:50.840
what's it going to be like?

00:14:50.840 --> 00:14:53.150
They put us in simulators,
and they shake the simulator

00:14:53.150 --> 00:14:54.890
a little bit, but
they said, forget it.

00:14:54.890 --> 00:14:56.970
That's nothing like
the real thing.

00:14:56.970 --> 00:14:59.270
It's hard to prepare yourself.

00:14:59.270 --> 00:15:02.690
It's an exciting time, the
last few minutes before launch,

00:15:02.690 --> 00:15:05.480
as the shuttle
really comes alive,

00:15:05.480 --> 00:15:08.390
and then all of a sudden you
get this big kick in the pants,

00:15:08.390 --> 00:15:10.400
and you look out the
window, and there's

00:15:10.400 --> 00:15:12.980
the ground falling away,
and everything is shaking,

00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:15.110
and tremendous noise.

00:15:15.110 --> 00:15:18.080
People told me that
there was going

00:15:18.080 --> 00:15:20.840
to be more vibration than
I could ever imagine,

00:15:20.840 --> 00:15:24.170
and they were right, because
I couldn't imagine it.

00:15:24.170 --> 00:15:27.350
45 seconds later, you're
going straight up,

00:15:27.350 --> 00:15:29.000
and you break the sound barrier.

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:31.822
And that gives you
even more vibration,

00:15:31.822 --> 00:15:33.530
because you get all
of these shock waves.

00:15:33.530 --> 00:15:35.900
And I remember
thinking to myself,

00:15:35.900 --> 00:15:37.790
no, this can't be normal.

00:15:37.790 --> 00:15:39.590
The wings are going to fall off.

00:15:39.590 --> 00:15:40.850
Something's wrong.

00:15:40.850 --> 00:15:43.083
But I mean, the
structural engineers

00:15:43.083 --> 00:15:44.750
knew what they were
doing, and the wings

00:15:44.750 --> 00:15:47.180
did not fall off-- or else
I wouldn't be here talking

00:15:47.180 --> 00:15:47.990
with you.

00:15:47.990 --> 00:15:52.010
With the Shuttle, it was those
two big, solid rocket boosters

00:15:52.010 --> 00:15:53.750
that caused all
of the vibration,

00:15:53.750 --> 00:15:56.150
and they burn for
the first 2 minutes.

00:15:56.150 --> 00:15:59.510
Once they fall off, it's
a really smooth ride.

00:15:59.510 --> 00:16:03.170
I mean, everybody then talks
about it's an electric ride

00:16:03.170 --> 00:16:05.540
all the way up into orbit.

00:16:05.540 --> 00:16:08.330
So that contrast between
the extreme vibration

00:16:08.330 --> 00:16:12.080
of riding on the side of
a solid rocket booster,

00:16:12.080 --> 00:16:16.130
and then just the burning of
the hydrogen-oxygen engines

00:16:16.130 --> 00:16:20.260
was really quite extraordinary.

00:16:20.260 --> 00:16:22.450
SARAH HANSEN: Did anyone
ever just get really scared

00:16:22.450 --> 00:16:25.055
right before lift off?

00:16:25.055 --> 00:16:26.680
JEFF HOFFMAN: There
are some astronauts

00:16:26.680 --> 00:16:28.450
who will tell you they
don't like launches

00:16:28.450 --> 00:16:31.150
because of the risk involved.

00:16:31.150 --> 00:16:34.240
For me-- I mean, I remember,
before the first flight,

00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:37.270
riding out to the launch
pad and looking up

00:16:37.270 --> 00:16:40.120
at this incredible vehicle
that I'm going to get in

00:16:40.120 --> 00:16:43.090
and thinking to
myself, first of all,

00:16:43.090 --> 00:16:45.250
this is not the time
to be asking yourself

00:16:45.250 --> 00:16:47.473
if you really want to do this.

00:16:47.473 --> 00:16:49.390
But then what was really
going through my mind

00:16:49.390 --> 00:16:51.670
is ever since I
was six years old

00:16:51.670 --> 00:16:55.510
and I saw Flash Gordon take off
in his rocket ship in science

00:16:55.510 --> 00:16:58.240
fiction, and I always
dreamed of doing it,

00:16:58.240 --> 00:17:01.540
and I'm about to
leave the planet--

00:17:01.540 --> 00:17:02.930
it was exciting.

00:17:02.930 --> 00:17:08.530
I mean, so my idea was sit
back and enjoy the ride.

00:17:08.530 --> 00:17:10.810
I'm not a pilot, so I
didn't have to even worry

00:17:10.810 --> 00:17:12.010
about flying the shuttle.

00:17:12.010 --> 00:17:14.500
I could just look out
the window and enjoy

00:17:14.500 --> 00:17:17.920
this incredible experience--
which it certainly was.

00:17:17.920 --> 00:17:22.240
Then all of a sudden, you
feel yourself floating up

00:17:22.240 --> 00:17:23.200
in your seat.

00:17:23.200 --> 00:17:25.000
You're weightless.

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:28.990
I remember unstrapping and
floating over to the window,

00:17:28.990 --> 00:17:32.680
and there was the coast of
Africa coming over the horizon,

00:17:32.680 --> 00:17:34.450
and that's when
it really hit me.

00:17:34.450 --> 00:17:35.260
Oh, my God.

00:17:35.260 --> 00:17:35.980
I'm in space!

00:17:40.263 --> 00:17:41.930
SARAH HANSEN: While
launching into space

00:17:41.930 --> 00:17:45.080
is certainly an incredible
ride, Professor Hoffman

00:17:45.080 --> 00:17:48.110
also did a number of
spacewalks during his career.

00:17:48.110 --> 00:17:50.960
He shared with us the
story of one of them.

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:53.480
JEFF HOFFMAN: I remember
my very first spacewalk

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:55.220
on my first space flight.

00:17:55.220 --> 00:17:59.990
I went out, and we had to
attach some special tools

00:17:59.990 --> 00:18:03.050
to the end of the robotic
arm as part of an attempt

00:18:03.050 --> 00:18:05.990
to rescue a satellite
which had been deployed

00:18:05.990 --> 00:18:07.610
but didn't turn on.

00:18:07.610 --> 00:18:11.670
And then we had been out
for about two hours or so,

00:18:11.670 --> 00:18:15.530
and the sun set, and then they
didn't have the right lighting

00:18:15.530 --> 00:18:16.100
conditions.

00:18:16.100 --> 00:18:19.010
And I'll never forget the call
that came up from the ground--

00:18:19.010 --> 00:18:21.380
hey, Jeff and Dave, would
you mind staying outside

00:18:21.380 --> 00:18:24.047
for another 45 minutes while
we wait for the sun to come--

00:18:24.047 --> 00:18:25.130
SARAH HANSEN: Oh, my gosh!

00:18:25.130 --> 00:18:26.713
JEFF HOFFMAN: And I
had nothing to do.

00:18:26.713 --> 00:18:29.540
I was just-- I mean, I was
a tourist out in a spacesuit

00:18:29.540 --> 00:18:30.500
holding on.

00:18:30.500 --> 00:18:33.720
I thought to myself, I'm
just going to have some fun.

00:18:33.720 --> 00:18:36.830
So actually, I was sort of
crawling all over the shuttle.

00:18:36.830 --> 00:18:43.220
And thinking back, I
crawled halfway up the tail,

00:18:43.220 --> 00:18:45.680
and the commander told
me he was doing his best

00:18:45.680 --> 00:18:47.450
to keep the TV cameras off me.

00:18:47.450 --> 00:18:49.490
I mean, I was so ecstatic.

00:18:49.490 --> 00:18:52.633
I was thinking about the
fact that maybe some people

00:18:52.633 --> 00:18:54.050
on the ground
wouldn't want to see

00:18:54.050 --> 00:18:56.160
an astronaut hanging from
the tail of the shuttle,

00:18:56.160 --> 00:18:56.780
but I did it.

00:19:02.350 --> 00:19:04.930
SARAH HANSEN: It's all
a matter of perspective.

00:19:04.930 --> 00:19:07.150
To broaden your own
horizons from the safety

00:19:07.150 --> 00:19:10.150
of your own home, be sure to
check out Professor Hoffman's

00:19:10.150 --> 00:19:12.970
course materials on
MIT OpenCourseWare,

00:19:12.970 --> 00:19:15.940
or on the MIT Open
Learning library.

00:19:15.940 --> 00:19:17.890
Thanks so much for listening.

00:19:17.890 --> 00:19:21.100
Your comments have inspired
me to keep learning.

00:19:21.100 --> 00:19:24.790
Until next time, signing off
from Cambridge, Massachusetts,

00:19:24.790 --> 00:19:27.850
I'm Sarah Hansen from
MIT OpenCourseWare.

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:40.650
AUDIO BUMPER: Ma, park the car.

